 time competing most people  who win or most people who enter are  older men  who are in the hunting community and i  was like oh well this hasn't really been  tapped by my generation it'd be cool to  be one of the youngest people a female  or non-binary or anything like that to  just participate  this year the 22 year old who lives near  minneapolis raced against the submission  deadline it's coming along nicely i  really like the wing there are nine days  but if i want to be realistic seven  fennell bringing followers along as they  documented their entry spent up to 100  hours painting it the tick tock clips  with cameos from the cat elton took off  introducing a new community to fettel's  work and the contest itself all right  duck people today is the last day both  eleanor and burley have been filled in  but i do still need to work on burley a  bit i thought  hunting art  it just didn't really mix with  the typical population on tick-tock but  they were like wow that's dorky that's  cool  i love it the new found viral fame has  brought with it an unexpected spike in  sales of their artwork fennel says  they've made thousands of dollars for  prints from their etsy account with  orders coming in from as far away as  australia and iceland just based off of  this tick tock yeah my  views and purchases and orders  definitely spiked right after that it's  like the 2021 version of the million  dollar duck yeah  all this before a winner has even been  decided a panel of judges will pour over  entries usually numbering more than 100  and make their pick later this month but  fennel says being able to raise  awareness of the contest has been  victory enough  i've definitely already won i've done so  much more that i even hoped to in the  beginning  and i'll just be excited for next year  for cbs mornings i'm niti chen in inver  grove heights minnesota  you
 

